+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`Flask.do_teardown_request` and `Flask.do_teardown_appcontext` currently call
+callbacks and their teardown signals directly, so an exception prevents later
+callbacks and receivers from running. `AppContext.pop` must reset its context
+variable even when either teardown phase fails.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+- Collect failures while continuing each teardown callback and signal receiver.
+- Reuse one small internal execution mechanism for both teardown paths.
+- Preserve existing callback ordering and exception arguments.
+- Raise collected failures only after context cleanup completes.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+- Change teardown registration, ordering, or normal signal semantics.
+- Suppress teardown failures or alter exceptions from request dispatch.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Represent teardown work as individual callables and execute it through a
+  shared internal helper that catches `BaseException`, records it, and raises
+  after all work has been attempted. This avoids duplicated, subtly divergent
+  request and app-context loops.
+- Dispatch signal receivers as individual teardown work items rather than
+  relying on a signal's bulk `send`, since bulk dispatch stops at a raising
+  receiver. Receiver lookup and invocation must preserve Flask's existing
+  sender and async-wrapper behavior.
+- Raise an exception group when the runtime provides it; otherwise raise the
+  first recorded failure. The helper returns only after all work ran, allowing
+  the existing `finally` in context popping to restore context-local state.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Signal receiver ordering differs from opaque signal bulk dispatch] → use the
+  signal library's established receiver iteration behavior and test that all
+  receivers run.
+- [A cleanup failure can mask an in-flight request exception] → this is the
+  settled teardown contract; retain the original exception as the `exc`
+  argument to every participant.
