@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Flask currently stops request or application-context teardown at the first
+callback or signal receiver that raises, leaving later cleanup work undone.
+Teardown must complete its registered cleanup work while still reporting all
+failures to the caller.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Run every request teardown callback and teardown signal receiver even when a
+  prior one raises.
+- Run every application-context teardown callback and teardown signal receiver
+  under the same rule.
+- Raise teardown failures only after cleanup and context-local restoration have
+  completed, preserving multiple failures as an exception group where Python
+  supports it.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+
+- `resilient-teardown-cleanup`: Reliable request and application-context
+  teardown execution and failure reporting.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+- None.
+
+## Impact
+
+Changes Flask's teardown execution in `src/flask/app.py` and adds regression
+coverage for request and application-context teardown behavior. No new
+dependencies or public registration APIs are introduced.
